@inject AppSession Session
@inject PaletteSwitch Palette
@implements IDisposable

@* The destinations that are not part of the daily loop, behind one disclosure.

   Shared by both navs rather than written twice. The tab bar had this list and the top nav did
   not, so the top nav carried all twelve links inline: at 1280px that needed 1242px of a 1265px
   row and the theme button dropped to a second line, and every width below that wrapped worse.
   Two copies of the list would also drift -- a new page added to one nav and not the other is a
   page that exists on a phone and not on a laptop.

   The id is a parameter because both navs are in the DOM at once, hidden from each other by
   width. Two popovers cannot share one id: popovertarget resolves to the first, so the tab bar's
   button would open the top nav's menu. *@

<div id="@Id" popover class="nav-more">
    @* The palette reaches everything this list does and a great deal more, so it goes first and is
       styled as an action rather than a destination. On a phone it is also the only way in: there
       is no Ctrl+K, and the top nav that carries the palette button is hidden at those widths. *@
    <button class="nav-more-search" @onclick="() => Palette.Toggle()">
        <span class="ico" aria-hidden="true">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="15" height="15" focusable="false">
                <circle cx="7" cy="7" r="4.4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" />
                <path d="M10.4 10.4 14 14" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linecap="round" />
            </svg>
        </span>
        Jump to a Card, Set, Deck or Action
    </button>

    <p class="nav-more-head" id="@($"{Id}-head")">Everything Else</p>
    <nav aria-labelledby="@($"{Id}-head")">
        @* A mark on every item, from the same component the two navs use.
           ==================================================================================
           All nine or none. Six of these have an obvious drawing and three did not, and a grid
           where six items carry a mark and three do not reads as a grid that failed to load --
           so the three had to be solved before any of them could be used. Each glyph names in
           NavIcon what it is and, where it matters, what was drawn first and thrown away.

           The two closest pairs in the finished set are Settings against the More button that
           opens this menu (three lines, with knobs and without) and Wishlist against Wonder Pick
           (a clipboard and a card). Both are told apart by one feature rather than by their
           silhouette, which is worth knowing if either is ever redrawn. *@
        @* First of the list, because it is the one that came OUT of the primary bar rather
           than never having been in it. *@
        <NavLink class="nav-more-item" href="chase">
            <NavIcon For="chase" Size="16" />Chases
        </NavLink>
        <NavLink class="nav-more-item" href="decks">
            <NavIcon For="decks" Size="16" />Decks
        </NavLink>
        <NavLink class="nav-more-item" href="wonder">
            <NavIcon For="wonder" Size="16" />Wonder Pick
        </NavLink>
        <NavLink class="nav-more-item" href="trades">
            <NavIcon For="trades" Size="16" />Trades
        </NavLink>
        <NavLink class="nav-more-item" href="board">
            <NavIcon For="board" Size="16" />Wishlist
        </NavLink>
        @* Only with something to compare: a link that always says "you have one collection" is a
           dead end. *@
        @if (Session.MultipleProfiles)
        {
            <NavLink class="nav-more-item" href="compare">
                <NavIcon For="compare" Size="16" />Compare
            </NavLink>
        }
        <NavLink class="nav-more-item" href="resources">
            <NavIcon For="resources" Size="16" />Resources
        </NavLink>
        <NavLink class="nav-more-item" href="history">
            <NavIcon For="history" Size="16" />History
        </NavLink>
        <NavLink class="nav-more-item" href="settings">
            <NavIcon For="settings" Size="16" />Settings
        </NavLink>
    </nav>

    @* Light dismiss alone left the phone with no way out that was not also a tap on the page: the
       sheet covers the bar it was opened from, so the only "outside" left to tap is the collection
       grid. A named way to close it, at the bottom where the thumb already is. Hidden at desk,
       where the opener stays visible and a dropdown with a Close button reads as a dialog. *@
    <button class="nav-more-close" popovertarget="@Id" popovertargetaction="hide">Close</button>
</div>

@* The other half of that fix. A popover's ::backdrop is not hit-testable, so the tap that light
   dismisses lands on whatever is under it -- opening a card the user was only trying to get away
   from. This takes the tap instead: it is outside the popover, so it still dismisses, and it is a
   real element, so nothing behind it is clicked. Sibling rather than a child, since a child would
   count as inside and dismiss nothing. *@
<div class="nav-more-scrim" aria-hidden="true"></div>

@code {
    /// <summary>DOM id the opening button points at with popovertarget. Unique per nav.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Id { get; set; } = "";

    protected override void OnInitialized() => Session.Changed += OnChanged;

    public void Dispose() => Session.Changed -= OnChanged;

    private void OnChanged() => InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);
}
